<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.2.3: Completing the Square</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02354</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.2.3: Completing the Square</md:title>
    <md:uuid>822d49f5-0fd1-43ba-a63d-13318e113f23</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">One technique for solving <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">quadratic equation</term>s is called <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">completing the square</term>. Here are two examples of how Diego and Mai completed the square to solve the same equation.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable" id="table-00001">
        <tgroup cols="2">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Diego</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Mai</emphasis>
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}+10 x+9 &amp;=0 \\ x^{2}+10 x &amp;=-9 \\ x^{2}+10 x+25 &amp;=-9+25 \\ x^{2}+10 x+25 &amp;=16 \\ (x+5)^{2} &amp;=16 \\ \sqrt{(x+5)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{16} \\ x+5 &amp;=\pm \;4 \\ \\ x+5=4 \text { or } x+5 &amp;=-4 \\ x=-1 \text { and } x=-9 &amp; \end{aligned}\)</entry>
              <entry>\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}+10 x+9 &amp;=0 \\ x^{2}+10 x+9+16 &amp;=16 \\ x^{2}+10 x+25 &amp;=16 \\ (x+5)^{2} &amp;=16 \\ \sqrt{(x+5)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{16} \\ (x+5) &amp;=\pm \;4 \\ \\ x+5=4 \text { and } x+5=-4 \\ x=-1 \text { or } x=-9 \end{aligned} \)</entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
      <para id="para-00002">Study the worked examples. Then, try solving these equations by completing the square for questions 1 – 5.</para>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">\(x^2+6x+8=0\)</para>
          <para id="para-00004">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00006">\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}+6 x+8 &amp;=0 \\ x^{2}+6 x &amp;=-8 \\ x^{2}+6 x+9 &amp;=-8+9 \\ (x+3)^{2} &amp;=1 \\ \sqrt{(x+3)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{1} \\ x+3 &amp;=\pm \;1 \\ x+3 &amp;=1 \\ x+3 &amp;=-1 \\ \\ x=-4 &amp; \text { and } x=-2 \end{aligned}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">\(x^2+12x=13\)</para>
          <para id="para-00008">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00010">\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}+12 x &amp;=13 \\ x^{2}+12 x+36 &amp;=13+36 \\ x^{2}+12 x+36 &amp;=49 \\ (x+6)^{2} &amp;=49 \\ \sqrt{(x+6)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{49} \\ x+6 &amp;=\pm \;7 \\ x+6 &amp;=7 \\ x+6 &amp;=-7 \\ \\ x=-13 &amp; \text { and } x=1 \end{aligned}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">\(0=x^2−10x+21\)</para>
          <para id="para-00012">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">\(\begin{aligned} 0=\; &amp; x^{2}-10 x+21 \\ x^{2}-10 x+21 &amp;=0 \\ x^{2}-10 x &amp;=-21 \\ x^{2}-10 x+25 &amp;=-21+25 \\ x^{2}-10 x+25 &amp;=4 \\ (x-5)^{2} &amp;=4 \\ \sqrt{(x-5)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{4} \\ x-5 &amp;=\pm \;2 \\ x-5 &amp;=2 \\ x-5 &amp;=-2 \\ \\ x=3 &amp; \text { and } x=7 \end{aligned}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">\(x^2−2x+3=83\)</para>
          <para id="para-00016">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00018">\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}-2 x+3 &amp;=83 \\ x^{2}-2 x &amp;=83-3 \\ x^{2}-2 x &amp;=80 \\ x^{2}-2 x+1 &amp;=80+1 \\ x^{2}-2 x+1 &amp;=81 \\ \sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{81} \\ x-1 &amp;=\pm \;9 \\ x-1 &amp;=9 \\ x-1 &amp;=-9 \\ \\ x=-8 &amp; \text { and } x=10 \end{aligned}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00019">\(x^2+40=14x\)</para>
          <para id="para-00020">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00022">\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}+40 &amp;=14 x \\ x^{2}-14 x+40 &amp;=0 \\ x^{2}-14 x &amp;=-40 \\ x^{2}-14 x+49 &amp;=-40+49 \\ x^{2}-14 x+49 &amp;=9 \\ \sqrt{(x-7)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{9} \\ x-7 &amp;=\pm \;3 \\ x-7 &amp;=3 \\ x-7 &amp;=-3 \\ \\ x=4 &amp; \text { and } x=10 \end{aligned}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
            <para id="para-00023">Here is a diagram made of a square and two congruent rectangles. Its total area is \(x^2+35x\) square units.</para>
            <para id="para-00024">Diagram A</para>
            <para id="para-00025">
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_10_04_06_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
            <para id="para-00026">What is the length of the unlabeled side of each of the two rectangles in Diagram A?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00027">Enter the length of the unlabeled side of each of the two rectangles.</para>
            <para id="para-00028">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00029">17.5</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End interaction-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00007">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00030">In Diagram B, we add lines to make the figure a square. The area of the square becomes a quadratic equation.</para>
            <para id="para-00031">
                      <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_10_04_06_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
            <para id="para-00032">Calculate the equation, then put that equation in the standard form \(ax^2+bx+c\) to answer questions a and b.</para>
            <para id="para-00033">Using the standard form \(ax^2+bx+c\) to represent the area of the square in Diagram B, what is the value of the coefficient \(b\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00034">Enter the value of the coefficient \(b\).</para>
            <para id="para-00035">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00036">35</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End interaction-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00008">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00037">Using the standard form \(ax^2+bx+c\) to represent the area of the square in Diagram B, what is the value of the constant \(c\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00038">Enter the value of the constant \(c\).</para>
            <para id="para-00039">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00040">306.25</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End interaction-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00009">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00041">How is the process of finding the area of the entire figure like the process of building perfect squares for expressions like \(x^2+bx\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00042">Enter your explanation of how the two processes are alike.</para>
            <para id="para-00043">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00044">First, we had to figure out what number was half of 35 (which is 17.5) to find the side length of the rectangles. Then, we had to square the number 17.5 to find the area of the missing piece.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Video: Solve Equations by Completing the Square</title>
        <para id="para-00045">Watch the following video to learn more about solving equations by completing the square.</para>
